@startuml mine-contact
' SPEC: _spec/packages/starchess-core/mine-contact.puml
' purpose: board/mine-contact.ts — the single spider-mine rules table every
' caller in the engine (and the Adjutant) asks. Authority: this is shipped.
' covers: packages/starchess-core/src/board/mine-contact.ts
' covers: packages/starchess-core/src/__tests__/mine/**
' see also: deploy-reach.puml (reuses the arrive row for deploy landings)
!include ../../themes/proveo.puml

skinparam packageStyle rectangle
skinparam defaultTextAlignment left

title StarChess Core · Mine contact — one table, every caller

package "board/mine-contact.ts" {
  component "mineContact({ mines, color, piece, squares, mode })\n→ inert | cleared(at, mineColor)\n     | detonated(at, mineColor, chaseFrom?)" as MC <<app>>
  component "withoutMine(mines, hit)\nspends ""chaseFrom"" when the wake was mid-path" as WM <<db>>
  component "hostileMineOn(mines, color, at)\nplain predicate — legality / AI avoidance" as HM <<db>>
}

package "callers" {
  component "resolve-turn.ts\nclearOnCapture · attack-clear · relocate walk\napproachMineContact (phase 3)" as RT <<app>>
  component "submit-turn.ts\napplyArrivalMine — gas step · deploy · jump" as SUB <<app>>
  component "board/moves.ts\nhostileMineOnTo (legality + UI affordance)" as MV <<app>>
  component "ai/adjutant.ts\nnever step / drop / jump onto a mine it sees" as AI <<host>>
  component "board/deploy-reach.ts\ndeployTargets — drop mined landings" as DR <<app>>
}

RT ARROW_MAIN MC
RT ARROW_MAIN WM
RT ARROW_OPTIONAL HM : marine diagonal target test
SUB ARROW_MAIN MC
SUB ARROW_MAIN WM
AI ARROW_MAIN HM
MV ARROW_OPTIONAL HM
DR ARROW_MAIN HM : a landing under a hostile mine is no landing

note right of MC
  **The table.** ""squares"" is the contact list **in walk order** — the last
  entry is the tile the unit ends on:

  | mode | who asks | outcome |
  | ""attack"" | a unit that fires at the tile (incl. every capture) | mine dies, mover lives → ""cleared"" |
  | ""walk"" | classical move / fog approach (ray + landing tile) | both die → ""detonated"" |
  | ""arrive"" | gas step · deploy · Tactical Jump | both die → ""detonated"" |
  | ""walkThenAttack"" | phase-3 fog approach that **captures** | crossed tiles walk, the landing is fired upon |

  **A king never trades with a mine.** Its contact is an ""attack"" in *every*
  mode, so the rule is stated once here instead of at each call site:
  * capturing a unit on a mined tile takes **both** the unit and the mine;
  * "walking over" a mine is an **attack/move** — the Command Center shoots the
    tile (""mineBlast"" + ""occupy"", via ""minedEmptyDestination"") and lands.
  **A blocker draws the shot; a mine never blocks.** That is why the landing tile
  of a capturing fog approach is ""attack"", not ""walk"": the unit standing there is
  what stopped the ray, so the shot lands on that tile and takes the mine under
  it, and the approaching unit occupies alive. Fog changes who *knew*, never who
  fired at what. Only tiles the approach **crossed** still detonate (that is the
  chase, with ""chaseFrom""). A ""kingShot"" approach stops *short* and fires from
  range, so the tile it settles on was never fired upon and stays a walk.

  **A capture reports the mine it took with it.** ""clearOnCapture"" returns the
  cleared colour and the caller stamps ""mineColor"" on its ""capture"" attack, so
  the timeline can bury two things on one tile: ""Rxe5"" onto a mined e5 projects
  the victim's death **and** a ""mine: true"" death beside it (""m"" in phase 1,
  ""prdm"" on an approach chain). Dropping that field is how the mine used to
  leave the board with no wreck — it applies to every capturer, not just the
  king (whose row above is the same rule).

  A king therefore never appears in ""detonated"". No mine survives under one
  either, and that holds **structurally**: a king lights its own 1-radius
  neighbourhood, so ""visible0"" is always true for its destination and it always
  reaches rule 8 (""minedEmptyDestination"") instead of the quiet path — and it
  leaps, so it is never the mover of a fog approach.
  Allied mines never match. An empty ""squares"" list is ""inert"".

  ""detonated.at"" is always the **landing** tile: a mine woken mid-path chases
  the mover and blows there, reporting the wake tile as ""chaseFrom"" (which is
  also the mine ""withoutMine"" spends).
end note

note as N_BLOCK
  **Mines are never blockers.** Check geometry (""attacksSquare"" via
  ""collectLegalKingChecks"") and ""firstBlockerOnRay"" — the pre-collected
  ""H:"" shot path in ""applyPendingKingShots"" — read the **placement map**
  only. A mine on a check ray therefore neither stops the shot nor is spent by
  it: this table is the **only** exit a mine has.

  An interposed **piece** on that same tile does both: it eats the shot
  (""collision"") and, being captured on a mined tile, clears the mine
  (""attack"" mode above). Pinned by ""src/__tests__/mine/mineChecks.test.ts"";
  the tutorial lesson ""king/king-attack-1"" plants c6 on the h1-a8 diagonal so
  the product board carries the same case.
end note

note as N_EXTRA_STEP
  **One walk per marine.** A Marine Extra Step makes the turn two hops, and the
  mine table is asked **once**, with the tile the marine comes to rest on as the
  landing — so a mine woken on the first hop *chases* onto the second exactly
  like a mid-slide wake. **Queue order cannot change the outcome:**

  | queue | first hop | second hop | who asks the table |
  | move-then-gas (""F"") | classical (phase 3) | Extra Step (phase 4) | phase 4 arrival, ""squares: [at, to]"" |
  | gas-then-move (""B"") | Extra Step (phase 1) | rewritten classical (phase 3) | phase 3 walk, ""squares: [extraStepFrom, …, dest]"" |

  Carried on ""ClassicalPlan"": ""extraStepTo"" makes phase 3 stand down (this is
  not the landing); ""extraStepFrom"" adds the tile the marine only reached this
  turn back onto the walk.

  ""endScTurn"" therefore **holds** a ""B"" gas step's arrival mine and collects
  its ""specialBefore"" event instead of logging it, because only
  ""rewriteClassicalAfterB"" can say whether the walk is handed on. The rewrite
  reads occupancy of the *continuation* tile, which a held blast can never
  change, so asking it first is exact rather than a guess; the event list is
  flushed straight after, keeping ""ev-<n>"" in queue order.

  **The walk still ends where it really ends.** A gas step with no classical to
  hand off to, or an Extra Step the board refuses (occupied / off-board), fires
  the held mine on the marine's actual resting tile.
end note

MC .. N_EXTRA_STEP
MC .. N_BLOCK

note bottom
  **What consolidating it fixed.** The same question used to be answered at
  fourteen ""mineAt"" reads in ""resolve-turn.ts"" plus a private copy in
  ""submit-turn.ts"" and three guards in the Adjutant. The halves drifted: gas
  steps, deploys and fog approaches silently skipped the rule until the arrival
  work landed (see ""src/__tests__/mine/mineContactRoutes.test.ts"").

  **Two deliberate corrections came with the table:**
  * a move that crosses **and** lands on mines now trips the **first** one it
    touches (ordered walk) — previously the landing tile was checked first;
  * king contact became an **attack in every mode** (see the table): capturing
    onto a mined tile takes the mine with the unit, and stepping onto one is
    ""mineBlast"" + occupy. The blanket ""piece === 'k'"" ⇒ ""inert"" short-circuit
    is gone; king *survival* is now expressed as "kings only ever ""cleared""".

  **The other two consolidations shipped with it:**
  * ""board/ray-scan.ts"" — the two duplicated ray walks in ""resolveTurn"" are
    one ""scanRay"" + one shared ""takeRayContact"" (""ray-scan.puml"");
  * ""endScTurn"" has a **single exit** (""finish(...)"") and an event log that
    owns ""ev-<n>"" sequencing — the three hand-written ""EndScTurnResult""
    literals and eleven ""events.push"" sites are gone.
  Together: ""resolve-turn.ts"" 1223 → ~890 lines, ""submit-turn.ts"" 1045 → ~848,
  with no test moved.

  **A fourth consolidation followed the same shape.** ""board/deploy-reach.ts""
  replaced a private, Marine-only deploy simulation inside
  ""ai/strategies/captures.ts"", and it reuses ""hostileMineOn"" here rather than
  re-deriving which landings a mine denies. Its own rule table is in
  ""deploy-reach.puml""; the row it borrows is ""arrive"".
end note
@enduml
